        "id": 260,
        "question": "Find the equation of the locus of a point P(x,y) which is equidistant from Q(0,0) and R(2,1).",
        "option": {
          "a": "4x + 2y = 5",
          "b": "4x - 2y = 5",
          "c": "2x + 2y = 5",
          "d": "2x + y = 5"
        },
        "section": "",
        "image": "",
        "answer": "a",
        "solution": "Locus of a point P(x,y) which is equidistant from Q(0,0) and R(2,1) is the perpendicular bisector of the straight line joining Q and R\nMid point QR = (x\u003Csup\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E+x\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E)/2,(y\u003Csub\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E+y\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E)/2\n\n= 2+0/2,1+0/2\n= (1,1/2)\n Gradient of QR = (y\u003Csub\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E−y\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E)/(x\u003Csub\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E−x\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E)\n= 1−0/2−0\n= 1/2\nGradient of PM = −1/(1/2)\n\n= -2\nEquation of PM = y - y\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E\n = m(x-x_1)\ni.e y - 1/2 = -2(x-1)\n2y - 1 = -4(x-1)\n2y - 1 = -4x + 4\n2y + 4x = 5",
        "examtype": "utme",
        "examyear": "2003"
      },
