        "solution": "3x + 2y + 4 = 0\nRearrange:\n\n2y = −3x − 4\nDivide both sides by 2\n\ny = (−3× − 4)/2\ny = −3/2×−2\n∴ the gradient of the line 3x + 2y + 4 = 0 is −3/2\nIf two lines are perpendicular to each other ∴ m\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003Exm\u003Csub\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E = -1\n\nLet m\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E =−3/2 ∴ m\u003Csub\u003E2\u003C/sub\u003E = −1/m\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E = −1/(−3/2) = 2/3\nFrom the equation of a line which is given as m = (y − y\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E)/x − x\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E) where (x\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E, y\u003Csub\u003E1\u003C/sub\u003E) = (2, 3)\n∴ 2/3 = (y − 3)/x − 2)\n=3(y  -  3) = 2(x  -  2)\n\n= 3y  -  9  =  2  x  -4\n\n= 3y  =  2  x  -4  +  9\n\n∴ 3y  =  2x  +  5",
